In-Reply-To: <soKh7FZ7ZVg.juEVmjJ24d4J@knitr.groups.google.com>
References: <soKh7FZ7ZVg.juEVmjJ24d4J@knitr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/knitr/c/soKh7FZ7ZVg/m/qvGId8G31vIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Unfortunately that is by design. Identical plots in the same code
chunk are removed. However, I do not see why you have to draw
identical plots. If you want to show a plot, then some new elements
added to the plot, you can keep the full history by fig.keep = 'all',
e.g.

<<fig.height=2, fig.width=2, fig.show='hold', fig.keep='all'>>=

par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0))
tmp <- rnorm(100)
plot(tmp)

axis(1,c(20,60,100))
@

This code chunk will generate two plots.

Regards,
Yihui
--
Yihui Xie <xiey...@gmail.com>
Web: http://yihui.name

On Sun, Nov 23, 2014 at 10:58 AM, dogle <do...@northland.edu> wrote:
> I am trying to create a document that demonstrates how axis() can be used to
> add axis labels to an existing plot.  I was hoping to create a plot and,
> using fig.show='hold',  have that same plot shown next to the original plot
> but followed with axis() to add the new axis labels.  For example, I have
> the following chunk.
>
> <<bad, fig.height=2, fig.width=2, fig.show='hold'>>=
> par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0))
> tmp <- rnorm(100)
> plot(tmp)
> plot(tmp)
> axis(1,c(20,60,100))
> @
>
> However, this simply produces one plot with the additional axis labels
> (i.e., the last plot produced).
>
> If the two calls to plot() have different data then I get the side-by-side
> plots.
>
> <<good1, fig.height=2, fig.width=2, fig.show='hold'>>=
> par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0))
> plot(rnorm(100))
> plot(rnorm(100))
> axis(1,c(20,60,100))
> @
>
> I know that I can get my desired side-by-side plots by including
> mfrow=c(1,2) in par() (shown below), but I am wondering if there is a knitr
> solution to my issue (rather than using par()).
>
> <<good2, fig.height=2, fig.width=4,fig.show='hold'>>=
> par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0),mfrow=c(1,2))
> tmp <- rnorm(100)
> plot(tmp)
> plot(tmp)
> axis(1,c(20,60,100))
> @
>
> p.s., I am using knitr v1.8, R v3.1.1, Rstudio 0.98.1062, and Windows.  The
> entire minimal example of the code above is shown below.
>
> \documentclass{article}
> \begin{document}
>
> I would like the plot repeated so I can demonstrate adding axis values.
>
> <<bad, fig.height=2, fig.width=2, fig.show='hold'>>=
> par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0))
> tmp <- rnorm(100)
> plot(tmp)
> plot(tmp)
> axis(1,c(20,60,100))
> @
>
> It works if the second plot is different.
> <<good1, fig.height=2, fig.width=2, fig.show='hold'>>=
> par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0))
> plot(rnorm(100))
> plot(rnorm(100))
> axis(1,c(20,60,100))
> @
>
> Or if I use multiple columns.
> <<good2, fig.height=2, fig.width=4,fig.show='hold'>>=
> par(mar=c(3,3,0.5,0.5),mgp=c(1.8,0.65,0),mfrow=c(1,2))
> tmp <- rnorm(100)
> plot(tmp)
> plot(tmp)
> axis(1,c(20,60,100))
> @
>
> \end{document}

